s also the \"ddmin\" algorithm that uses a recursive approach. Here it says \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That suggests after a successful removal, we make the granularity coarser (fewer chunks) so we test larger chunks again. \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" means if none of the current chunks can be removed, we make the granularity finer (more chunks). And stop at singleton granularity, meaning when granularity is 1 (i.e., each chunk is a single item), we stop.\n\nSo granularity could be the number of chunks. Starting at 2. After a successful removal, we want \"slightly coarser granularity\" - maybe we set granularity back to 2? Or increment by 1? \"Slightly coarser\" might mean increase by 1? But then doubling would be more aggressive. Possibly the intended algorithm: maintain a current granularity g (number of chunks). Initially g=2. Then while g > 1 (or until g=1): For each chunk index i from 0 to g-1 (left to right), consider removing that chunk. If after removal the required failure items remain, then accept removal (i.e., update items to be the complement of that chunk), and then restart with g = max(2, g-1) or something? Actually \"slightly coarser granularity\" might mean increase chunk size, i.e., decrease g. So after successful removal, set g = max(2, g-1). Then start over. If no removal succeeds, double g (finer granularity) and try again. When g reaches len(items) (i.e., each chunk is size 1), that's singleton granularity, stop. But we need to define what happens when g is larger than current length? Possibly the algorithm should handle that.\n\nAlternatively, \"granularity\" could be the chunk size (number of items per chunk). Start with chunk size 2. Try removing each consecutive chunk of size 2. If a removal is successful, restart with chunk size 1? \"Slightly coarser\" would be larger chunk size, so after success you increase chunk size by 1? But then doubling when no removal succeeds would increase chunk size further. That seems backward: typically you start with large chunks (coarse) and then make them smaller (finer) if no removal works. Here they start at granularity two (maybe that's small? \"two\" could be small chunks). Actually \"starting at granularity two\" could mean chunks of size 2. Then if a removal succeeds, you want \"slightly coarser granularity\" -> larger chunks, e.g., size 3, 4, etc. And if no removal succeeds, you double granularity -> even larger chunks? That would go to size 4, 8, etc. That doesn't make sense because larger chunks are less likely to be removable. Usually we start coarse and go finer. So I think granularity is the number of chunks, so start with 2 chunks (coarse), then if removal succeeds, go even coarser (1 chunk) - but that would be the whole set, which is not useful. Actually after removing a chunk, the set is smaller, so perhaps we go back to granularity 2? Or maybe \"slightly coarser\" means set granularity back to 2? Or increment by 1? The phrase \"slightly coarser\" suggests a small increase in chunk size, i.e., decrease in number of chunks.\n\nLet's examine the described algorithm from a known delta debugging variant: The \"ddmin\" algorithm works as follows: Start with n=2. Split into n subsets. Test each subset (complement). If a subset is found, reduce to complement and set n = max(2, n-1) (coarser). If no subset found, increase n = min(len(current), 2*n) (finer). Continue until n equals len(current) (each subset size 1). That matches \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" (2*n) and \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" (n-1). \"Stop at singleton granularity\" when n == len(current) i.e., each subset is a singleton. Yes, that is a standard delta debugging algorithm. So we should implement that.\n\nThus: given a list of items, we want to find a minimal subset that still contains all required_failure_items. The interestingness oracle here is whether the required_failure_items remain. So we have a function `interesting(items_subset)` that returns True if all required_failure_items are in the subset. We want to reduce the list while preserving that property.\n\nAlgorithm steps:\n- Start with current set = case['items'] list.\n- required = set(case['required_failure_items'])\n- n = 2 (number of chunks)\n- While n > 1 and len(current) > len(required)? Actually stop when n == len(current) (singleton granularity) but also when no further reduction possible? The algorithm typically stops when n >= len(current) because each chunk is size 1 and no removal works. So we can loop while n < len(current) or while n <= len(current)? Let's implement as described.\n\nI'll implement:\n- current = list of items\n- required_set = set(required_failure_items)\n- attempts = 0\n- n = 2\n- while n <= len(current):\n  - partition current into n roughly equal chunks (or as equal as possible). Important: chunks are contiguous? The description says \"Try chunks from left to right\". So we partition consecutively.\n  - For each chunk index i from 0 to n-1:\n    - Create candidate = current without that chunk (i.e., complement)\n    - attempts += 1\n    - Check if all required items are in candidate (i.e., required_set.issubset(candidate))\n    - If yes:\n      - current = candidate\n      - # restart with slightly coarser granularity: n = max(2, n-1)\n      - n = max(2, n-1)  # or n = n-1? but ensure >=2\n      - break out of chunk loop and restart outer loop (while)\n  - If no chunk removal succeeded:\n    - n = min(len(current), 2*n)  # double granularity, but not more than len(current)\n- After loop, we have reduced current.\n- Also we need to return reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check. One-minimal means that no single item can be removed while preserving the property. So we need to check for each item in current, removing it would lose a required item. We can compute that: for each item in current, if that item is not in required_set, we can consider if removing it still leaves all required? Actually one-minimal: the set is minimal such that removing any one element from it causes the loss of some required item? Or more generally, removing any single element results in failure (i.e., missing required item). But we need to check that every single element is necessary for preserving the required items. Since required items themselves are necessary (removing a required item would lose it), so they are automatically necessary. For non-required items, they might be removable individually? In a truly one-minimal reduction, no single item can be removed without losing a required item. So we need to verify that for each item in current, if we remove it, the required set is not fully contained. That is a property to return. So we can compute a boolean `one_minimal` that is True if for every i in range(len(current)), the set current[:i] + current[i+1:] does not contain all required items. Or simply check if any non-required item could be removed while still having all required. If such exists, then it's not one-minimal. But the algorithm should produce a one-minimal set? The description says \"Return the reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check.\" So we need to compute and include that check. The check is a boolean indicating whether the reduced set is one-minimal.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Input case has payload with 'items' (list of strings) and 'required_failure_items' (list of strings).\n- We must not mutate case.\n- Use only standard library.\n- Return a dict with keys: 'reduced_items', 'attempts', 'one_minimal'.\n\nLet's code this.\n\nEdge cases: \n- If len(items) is 0? but likely not.\n- If required_failure_items not all present in items? Assume they are.\n- When generating chunks, we need to ensure we try left to right. With n chunks, we split the list into n contiguous parts as evenly as possible. For each index i, the complement is the list without that chunk. We need to compute that correctly.\n- The algorithm: while n <= len(current): for each chunk, if removal works, update current, set n = max(2, n-1) and break. After loop if no removal, n = min(len(current), 2*n). Continue until n > len(current) (i.e., each chunk would be size 1? Actually when n == len(current), each chunk is exactly one element. At that point, we test each singleton removal. If none succeeds, then we double n to 2*len(current) which is > len(current), so loop condition fails and we stop. That's correct.\n- The attempts count: increment for each candidate tested, including those that fail.\n\nOne-minimal check: After reduction, test each element removal. If any removal (including required items) still contains all required? That can't happen because removing a required item would lose it. So only non-required items could potentially be removable. So we iterate over current, for each index, construct candidate list without that element, check if required_set.issubset(candidate). If any such candidate succeeds, then one_minimal = False. Else True.\n\nReturn dict.\n\nWe need to output JSON with key \"implementation\" and value as Python source code as a string. We must escape newlines, quotes, backslashes.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nI'll produce:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    items = case['payload']['items']\\n    required = set(case['payload']['required_failure_items'])\\n    current = list(items)\\n    n = 2\\n    attempts = 0\\n    while n <= len(current):\\n        # compute chunk sizes\\n        chunk_size = len(current) // n\\n        remainder = len(current) % n\\n        start = 0\\n        chunks = []\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            size = chunk_size + (1 if i < remainder else 0)\\n            if size == 0:\\n                # should not happen if n <= len\\n                break\\n            chunks.append(current[start:start+size])\\n            start += size\\n        # if chunks list incomplete due to